# A strict full-source competitor for parallel unit contacts

**Date:** 2026-10-11

**Base commit:** `a5f605b7` (state base `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`)

**State version:** `2026-10-10.audit.234`

**Target:** independent whole-source Level I research

**Run type:** closing-proof research; exact finite source move

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Dependencies: the problem definition and elementary finite Euclidean geometry
only. No KKT, selected stress, contact-minimality, secondary diameter-pair
minimality, source-core reduction, or other worker's proof is used.

## 1. Exact statement and implication chain

Let `A={p_1,...,p_n}` be a finite planar unit-separated source of diameter
`D>1`. Suppose all pairs at distance one are parallel to one fixed direction.
The case of no unit contacts is permitted. There exist arbitrarily close
placements `A_t` retaining **every label**, with

\[
\min_{i<j}|p_i(t)-p_j(t)|>1,
\qquad\operatorname{diam}(A_t)<D.                           \tag{1}
\]

In particular no globally minimizing source of diameter greater than one has
all unit contacts parallel. Combined with elementary diameter-one geometry,
no global minimizer of any cardinality `n>=3` has parallel unit contacts.

```text
parallel contact hypothesis
 -> explicit full-source strict sub-D competitor
 -> exclusion of this entire class of global minimizers
```

This does not exclude triangle-free sources with several contact directions.

## 2. Construction

Rotate coordinates so the contact direction is horizontal. Its contact graph
is a union of unit-step paths and isolates: each vertex has at most one
neighbor on each side, and a finite collinear component cannot be a cycle.
Give each component a two-coloring `s_i in {-1,+1}`, alternating along every
contact. Give isolates either sign.

Let `H` be the **complete actual diameter-pair set**, including pairs incident
to contact isolates. Choose an integer `K>=1` such that

\[
K|y_i-y_j|>2
\quad\text{for every }ij\in H\text{ with }y_i\ne y_j.       \tag{2}
\]

This is possible because `H` is finite; when it has no nonhorizontal pair,
take `K=1`. Define the simultaneous source motion

\[
\boxed{x_i(t)=(1-2t^2)x_i,
\qquad y_i(t)=(1-Kt)y_i+t s_i.}                            \tag{3}
\]

It is not an isometry of the contact framework. Its intended effect is to
open all contacts, rather than preserve them.

## 3. Every old contact opens

For an old contact, `|x_i-x_j|=1`, `y_i-y_j=0`, and `|s_i-s_j|=2`. Its new
squared distance is exactly

\[
(1-2t^2)^2+4t^2=1+4t^4>1\qquad(t>0).                     \tag{4}
\]

This simultaneously handles all contacts, including consecutive contacts
within the same path. No one-at-a-time compatibility assertion is used.

## 4. Every old diameter strictly shortens

For an old diameter write `a=x_i-x_j`, `b=y_i-y_j`, and `d=s_i-s_j`, so
`a^2+b^2=D^2` and `|d|<=2`.

If `b=0`, the exact squared change is

\[
\Delta_{ij}(t)=(-4D^2+d^2)t^2+4D^2t^4
\le4(1-D^2)t^2+4D^2t^4<0                                \tag{5}
\]

whenever `t^2<(D^2-1)/D^2`. This is where the hypothesis `D>1` enters.

If `b!=0`, put `z=Kb-d`. Equation (2) implies `b*z>0`. The squared change is

\[
\Delta_{ij}(t)=-2bz t+z^2t^2-4a^2t^2+4a^2t^4.            \tag{6}
\]

For `0<t<1/(2K)` the first two terms have negative sum. Indeed

\[
\frac{2|b|}{|z|}\ge\frac{2|b|}{K|b|+2}>\frac1K>t.
\]

For `t<1` the last two terms are nonpositive. Hence (6) is strictly
negative for every nonhorizontal old diameter, for one common `t`.

No KKT-weighted average replaces the maximum-diameter check: (5) and (6)
cover each member of the full actual set `H` individually.

## 5. All inactive full-source pairs remain feasible

Choose an integer `M>=D`. For `0<t<=1`, the change of any pair vector in
(3) has norm at most

\[
2t^2|a|+Kt|b|+t|d|\le E t,
\qquad E=(K+2)M+2.
\]

Consequently its squared-length change has absolute value at most

\[
B t,\qquad B=2ME+E^2.                                   \tag{7}
\]

Let `delta` be the minimum of all positive lower and upper slacks

\[
|p_i-p_j|^2-1\quad(ij\text{ not an old contact}),
\qquad D^2-|p_i-p_j|^2\quad(ij\notin H).
\]

If this collection is empty take `delta=1`. In every case `delta>0`.
Choose a common positive `t` satisfying

\[
t<\min\left\{1,\frac1{2K},
\sqrt{\frac{D^2-1}{D^2}},\frac{\delta}{2B}\right\}.        \tag{8}
\]

The inactive lower and upper slacks remain positive by (7). Together with
(4)--(6), this proves (1) for the entire source, including isolates. Taking
arbitrarily small such `t` proves the closeness assertion. Rescaling by the
new minimum distance, if exact normalization is desired, further reduces the
diameter. QED.

For completeness, if a unit-separated source has diameter one, every distinct
pair is at distance one. Three such points are an equilateral triangle and
their three contacts are not parallel; four such planar points cannot exist.
Thus the diameter-one exception contains no `n>=3` parallel-contact source.

## 6. Attainment consequence and remaining global gap

The companion
[`parallel-contact approximation report`](global_attainment_and_parallel_contact_density_2026-10-11.md)
proves `inf diam(A)=D(n)` even over parallel-path-contact sources. This report
shows that for `n>=3` that infimum is **never attained** in that class.
Together these give a fully explicit example of why an equality of restricted
and unrestricted diameter infima supplies no optimal source.

The global clearance formulation in the companion report still requires
`eta_n=0` for every sufficiently large `n`. Several contact directions defeat
the present motion: the linear transverse contraction in (3) can shorten
nonhorizontal contacts. Therefore (3) supplies no general triangle-free
competitor and no C-II closure.

## 7. Exact certificate and lifecycle

`research/verify_global_parallel_contact_competitor_2026_10_11.py` checks
horizontal chains, separated rows with an isolate, a stretched square, and a
stretched triangular wheel using `Q(sqrt(3))`. It enumerates every pair,
constructs the complete original contact and diameter sets, checks the actual
coloring and all bounds in (8), and certifies **strict** lower and upper
inequalities at the simultaneous endpoint. It rejects the diameter-one and
multiple-contact-direction fixtures. It does not certify general-source
hypotheses, global optimality of fixtures, or Level I.

This is an unpromoted candidate with a complete elementary proof. Preserve the
research checkpoint; do not change the canonical theorem status.
